WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.340
JOE: The level of an individual’s autism
disorder can be a factor in making a

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:09.709
decision about education, as those who are
high functioning may fit more easily into

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:14.280
mainstream schools but may not get that
extra attention they might thrive from.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.039
Some mainstream schools have centres
providing specialist support incorporated

00:00:19.039 --> 00:00:22.960
into them, this can provide support
from trained staff, such as learning

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.560
in small groups and therapies,
and a calm setting to retreat to.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:30.130
BETH: Before parents choose a school,
they should check up on what extra

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:34.180
facilities are available such as extra
teaching support, different approaches

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:35.940
to teaching and separate lessons.

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:40.800
This may be all a child with autism may
need, but for some this may not be enough.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.420
There are different benefits and
challenges to all of these types of

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.130
education, such as inclusion and good
practice, that are crucial for enabling

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:53.309
children on the autism spectrum to be
educated to their greatest potential.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.690
JOE: Once an individual with autism
has progressed through education, they

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.670
are going to have to gain employment
if they are going to be able to become

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.510
more independent and self-sufficient.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:07.080
Those who find employment may not always
be able to maintain this due to their

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.099
disorders and associated problems.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.259
However, if employers are more
aware of the issues and work with

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.420
occupational health professionals
often employers can benefit greatly

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.009
from employees on the spectrum.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.880
BETH: The nature of their disorder in
some high functioning individuals with

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:27.289
autism can make certain professions
ideal, such as using finely tuned

00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:30.959
special interests such as maths
abilities in finance and banking.

00:01:32.169 --> 00:01:34.426
Many local initiatives are now evolving,
helping companies to see an individual

00:01:34.426 --> 00:01:40.339
with autism as an asset to their
company rather than having a disability.

00:01:41.139 --> 00:01:44.619
JOE: Some individuals with autism can
struggle to get access to services as

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:48.979
they can be very articulate and seem
to show that they don’t need support

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.520
or the help of different services.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.830
BETH: They therefore can be blocked
from some social care services

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.220
or from learning disability or
mental health specific services.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:01.839
Social care assessors can sometimes
lack sufficient awareness of autism to

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.569
identify an individual’s specific needs.

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:09.400
JOE: To overcome any of these potential
effects an individual with autism

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:13.350
and their family, have more hurdles
to deal with on top of the nature of

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:17.890
their disorder, and this only make
life more difficult at every stage.

